- Oh, thank you.

00:02:40.677 --> 00:02:42.210
Absolutely.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.178
I'd like to start
discussions by asking people,

00:02:45.178 --> 00:02:46.346
what was your journey?

00:02:46.346 --> 00:02:49.624
How did you come to work in this field

00:02:49.624 --> 00:02:52.934
of test preparation and education?

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:54.102
- Well, honestly,

00:02:54.102 --> 00:02:57.000
I graduated college
without a really clear plan

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.814
of what I was gonna do.

00:02:58.814 --> 00:03:00.996
And I followed a friend to the DC

00:03:00.996 --> 00:03:03.796
and I took a job with a
local test prep company

00:03:03.796 --> 00:03:05.093
who said they were looking for folks

00:03:05.093 --> 00:03:06.676
who are good at standardized tests

00:03:06.676 --> 00:03:08.404
and like working with
high school students.

00:03:08.404 --> 00:03:10.204
And I'd always been pretty
good at standardized tests.

00:03:10.204 --> 00:03:12.598
And at that point, was pretty
nearly a high school student.

00:03:12.598 --> 00:03:15.079
And I thought, well, gosh,
I can give this a try.

00:03:15.079 --> 00:03:17.959
And I just, I really fell in love

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:19.458
with talking with teenagers.

00:03:19.458 --> 00:03:24.392
I just, it's such, Paul
Tuff in his wonderful book,

00:03:24.392 --> 00:03:26.641
"The Years That Matter Most,"
now "The Inequality Machine,"

00:03:26.641 --> 00:03:28.528
talks about the first year of college,

00:03:28.528 --> 00:03:30.985
but I think it also applies
to last year of high school

00:03:30.985 --> 00:03:33.685
of sort of the uncertainty
when he describes

00:03:33.685 --> 00:03:34.859
the belonging uncertainty.

00:03:34.859 --> 00:03:37.610
And so during those transitions,
kids are vulnerable.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.678
They're vulnerable to stress,

00:03:38.678 --> 00:03:41.884
but they're also vulnerable
to new ideas about themselves,

00:03:41.884 --> 00:03:45.052
and there's just something
really cool for me, at least,

00:03:45.052 --> 00:03:46.802
about working one-on-one with kids.

00:03:46.802 --> 00:03:48.600
And I'm not mom and dad,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.396
I'm not asking them to clean the room,

00:03:51.396 --> 00:03:52.739
I'm not a teacher giving grades.

00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:55.563
I'm just all about what
trying to help them

00:03:55.563 --> 00:03:57.128
in all the ways that
are meaningful to them.

00:03:57.128 --> 00:03:58.343
So I've been,

00:03:58.343 --> 00:04:00.098
I think something like 50,000 hours,

00:04:00.098 --> 00:04:02.759
one-on-one talking with kids-
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:02.759 --> 00:04:06.274
- since the early 90s and
it's just, it never gets old.

00:04:06.274 --> 00:04:07.961
It's really cool.

00:04:07.961 --> 00:04:10.119
- That is fantastic.

00:04:10.119 --> 00:04:13.628
So you write and talk a lot about stress.

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:15.471
Let's start there.
- I do.

00:04:15.471 --> 00:04:17.138
- Is all stress bad?

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.330
Are there should be thinking-
- No.

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.989
- about stress in different
ways at different times?

00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:27.230
- So yeah, so first of
all, all stress is not bad.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.643
Classically, when we talk about stress,

00:04:30.643 --> 00:04:32.228
there's something called
the Yerkes-Dodson curve,

00:04:32.228 --> 00:04:33.952
which some of your
educators may know about it.

00:04:33.952 --> 00:04:36.192
It's a bell curve, it's
a performance curve.

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:38.917
And it's really measured
in physiological arousal.

00:04:38.917 --> 00:04:40.597
So kind of what are those stress hormones?

00:04:40.597 --> 00:04:41.475
What are the neurotransmitters

00:04:41.475 --> 00:04:43.718
that are running through
our bodies and brains?

00:04:43.718 --> 00:04:45.880
And if we have no stress,

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.875
typically there's not much
by way of performance.

00:04:47.875 --> 00:04:50.316
If you play against a
team who's just terrible,

00:04:50.316 --> 00:04:52.558
you're gonna win, but you're
not gonna play your best.

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:55.650
At some point, you start
to feel butterflies, right?

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:57.794
Before a big game, before big math test,

00:04:57.794 --> 00:04:58.843
you're trying to talk to somebody

00:04:58.843 --> 00:04:59.921
who thinks you're really cute.

00:04:59.921 --> 00:05:01.165
You should feel that

00:05:01.165 --> 00:05:03.178
because it means that
you're paying attention.

00:05:03.178 --> 00:05:04.525
It means that your primed for action.

00:05:04.525 --> 00:05:06.275
It means that you're ready.

00:05:06.275 --> 00:05:09.461
The challenges that more
isn't necessarily better,

00:05:09.461 --> 00:05:10.990
and so at some point, ah,

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.469
then the performance starts to fall apart.

00:05:13.469 --> 00:05:15.401
And so, what we're trying to talk about

00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:17.166
is what helps any of us,

00:05:17.166 --> 00:05:18.191
but especially kids,

00:05:18.191 --> 00:05:21.934
feel like their place of what's
known as optimal arousal.

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:23.588
You're amped up enough to be excited,

00:05:23.588 --> 00:05:27.504
but not so much that you
kind of lose your mind.

00:05:27.504 --> 00:05:29.074
- That makes sense, it aligns to,

00:05:29.074 --> 00:05:32.809
I've also read some about the impact

00:05:32.809 --> 00:05:35.151
of stress hormones on memory

00:05:35.151 --> 00:05:38.150
and that a low, some
level of stress hormones

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.116
can actually help you remember more,

00:05:40.116 --> 00:05:41.700
but then it gets too high

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.699
and then you lose that.

00:05:43.699 --> 00:05:45.249
- Well, yeah, and especially, you know,

00:05:45.249 --> 00:05:46.386
one of the things that,

00:05:46.386 --> 00:05:48.686
so my coauthor, William Stixrud,

00:05:48.686 --> 00:05:50.659
he's a clinical neuropsychologist.

00:05:50.659 --> 00:05:53.898
So he often works with kids
where things aren't going well,

00:05:53.898 --> 00:05:54.986
you know, learning isn't easy,

00:05:54.986 --> 00:05:55.919
attention isn't easy,

00:05:55.919 --> 00:05:58.218
maybe emotional regulation isn't easy.

00:05:58.218 --> 00:06:00.454
And both for him as a neuropsychologist,

00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:02.435
and me as a test prep geek,

00:06:02.435 --> 00:06:05.039
we would submit the most
important outcome of high schools

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:06.380
not where you go to college.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.511
And I don't know, all
my clients are good one,

00:06:08.511 --> 00:06:10.634
but seriously, it's developing the brain

00:06:10.634 --> 00:06:12.395
that kids are gonna carry into college

00:06:12.395 --> 00:06:14.508
and then to adulthood.

00:06:14.508 --> 00:06:17.391
And cortisol, the dominant stress hormone,

00:06:17.391 --> 00:06:18.895
again, we need enough of it

00:06:18.895 --> 00:06:20.497
to kind of get us out
of bed in the morning,

00:06:20.497 --> 00:06:23.465
but too much it erodes the hippocampus,

00:06:23.465 --> 00:06:25.389
the major memory center of the brain,

00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:28.120
it weakens the connection
between the prefrontal cortex,

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.291
you know, the kind of
thinking part of our brain

00:06:30.291 --> 00:06:31.526
and the amygdala,

00:06:31.526 --> 00:06:36.230
and if you're too tired and
too stressed for too long,

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.322
very bad things happen
to developing brains.

00:06:38.322 --> 00:06:40.659
And we just don't wanna see young people,

00:06:40.659 --> 00:06:43.492
particularly teenagers, who
really sculpting the brains

00:06:43.492 --> 00:06:44.577
that will carry into adulthood.

00:06:44.577 --> 00:06:47.528
And we just don't wanna see
them be stressed all the time.

00:06:47.528 --> 00:06:50.799
Find to have intense in
you, work hard, relax hard,

00:06:50.799 --> 00:06:52.356
work hard, relax hard.

00:06:52.356 --> 00:06:53.702
That's kind of, just like working out,

00:06:53.702 --> 00:06:55.430
that's how you get stronger,

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:56.734
and we want kids to have that experience.

00:06:56.734 --> 00:06:59.800
But if they're constant
like this all the time,

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:01.727
just not good for developing brains,

00:07:01.727 --> 00:07:05.140
or for test performance for that matter.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:05.973
- Right, right.

00:07:05.973 --> 00:07:08.379
So what are signs that
someone who's too stressed

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:11.462
and those stress levels are too high?

00:07:12.695 --> 00:07:13.645
- Oh, golly.

00:07:13.645 --> 00:07:15.432
I mean, there are all sorts of symptoms

00:07:15.432 --> 00:07:18.412
when you talk about diagnosed anxiety.

00:07:18.412 --> 00:07:21.495
I mean, it's the words they're using,

00:07:23.041 --> 00:07:25.322
it's the constant fretfulness,

00:07:25.322 --> 00:07:27.658
it's perfectionism run them up,

00:07:27.658 --> 00:07:29.023
it's difficulty sleeping,

00:07:29.023 --> 00:07:31.484
it's difficult with appetite,
you know, with depression.

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:33.464
I mean, I'm not a clinical psychologist,

00:07:33.464 --> 00:07:35.797
but if you really have a kid

00:07:37.208 --> 00:07:38.518
who you're worried about mental health,

00:07:38.518 --> 00:07:41.227
just go to some of the websites

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:42.462
that we'll talk about these things,

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:44.256
but classically,

00:07:44.256 --> 00:07:47.213
but assuming that's just
garden variety anxiety, right?

00:07:47.213 --> 00:07:49.997
Which we all experience to a degree,

00:07:49.997 --> 00:07:53.449
but I really have become an interested in,

00:07:53.449 --> 00:07:56.096
for years kind of what I
think it was underperformance.

00:07:56.096 --> 00:07:58.424
So when I first started doing test prep,

00:07:58.424 --> 00:08:00.051
again I was always pretty
good at these tests,

00:08:00.051 --> 00:08:02.604
I was trained really well on
how to talk about these tests,

00:08:02.604 --> 00:08:04.546
but I kept having the experience

00:08:04.546 --> 00:08:07.330
of really bright kids
from their great families,

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:08.163
their great schools,

00:08:08.163 --> 00:08:09.179
they did all the hard work,

00:08:09.179 --> 00:08:11.779
and they go and take
the test and go, bush.

00:08:11.779 --> 00:08:15.379
And typically, I'd get an irate
phone call from the parents,

00:08:15.379 --> 00:08:20.379
I don't understand, (speaking gibberish)

00:08:20.388 --> 00:08:23.382
I didn't know anything when
I was 23, 24 doing this,

00:08:23.382 --> 00:08:24.868
but I was fortunate enough

00:08:24.868 --> 00:08:26.763
the parents would send their kids back

00:08:26.763 --> 00:08:28.944
and we'd have another conversation.

00:08:28.944 --> 00:08:29.796
And so, I'd asked them, you know,

00:08:29.796 --> 00:08:31.205
did the proctor give up the time?

00:08:31.205 --> 00:08:33.639
Was the kid next to you to distract?

00:08:33.639 --> 00:08:34.596
What it looked like?

00:08:34.596 --> 00:08:36.117
And almost always the
conversation would end

00:08:36.117 --> 00:08:37.061
with something like,

00:08:37.061 --> 00:08:38.736
I don't know,

00:08:38.736 --> 00:08:40.891
there was so much more than
what we did for practice.

00:08:40.891 --> 00:08:43.251
And so, oh, okay, that makes sense.

00:08:43.251 --> 00:08:44.489
Oh, wait, wait a second,
wait, wait, wait, wait,

00:08:44.489 --> 00:08:47.057
for all the reasons that
people may not like the SAT,

00:08:47.057 --> 00:08:49.599
or may not like the ACT,

00:08:49.599 --> 00:08:51.679
that just don't like
standardized tests generally,

00:08:51.679 --> 00:08:55.280
you have to give them
this, they're standardized.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.440
So the idea, right,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.231
that this test that they took
was so fundamentally different

00:08:59.231 --> 00:09:02.066
than what they'd done for
the books of practice tests,

00:09:02.066 --> 00:09:03.157
just didn't make a lot of sense.

00:09:03.157 --> 00:09:05.210
So I went to go and take the SAT

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:06.674
at a local public high school,

00:09:06.674 --> 00:09:08.130
and I'm 25, 26, you know,

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:11.021
I was probably five years into
being the test post late 20s.

00:09:11.021 --> 00:09:13.525
So I'm sitting there
with all these teenagers

00:09:13.525 --> 00:09:14.520
and kind of eavesdropping,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.176
I'm like, what's going
through their heads?

00:09:16.176 --> 00:09:18.123
And are they nervous or anxious, whatever.

00:09:18.123 --> 00:09:20.657
And this is that I wrote
a book years ago about,

00:09:20.657 --> 00:09:22.214
and Khan called "Conquering the SAT"

00:09:22.214 --> 00:09:25.045
about anxiety and test performance.

00:09:25.045 --> 00:09:28.584
And for no particular reason,
I checked my own pulse.

00:09:28.584 --> 00:09:31.904
It's 100, it was 140 beats a minute,

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:33.845
140 beats a minutes-
- Oh my gosh.

00:09:33.845 --> 00:09:35.926
- which is like running up
and down stairs 15 times.

00:09:35.926 --> 00:09:37.269
And I thought, this is curious

00:09:37.269 --> 00:09:40.446
'cause I didn't think that I was nervous

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:43.178
and I have very few identifiable skills,

00:09:43.178 --> 00:09:44.052
but one of them

00:09:44.052 --> 00:09:46.039
is that I've always been
good at standardized tests.

00:09:46.039 --> 00:09:47.371
So like, and I didn't need it

00:09:47.371 --> 00:09:48.523
'cause I wasn't going back to college,

00:09:48.523 --> 00:09:50.486
like what was going on?

00:09:50.486 --> 00:09:54.317
And so a lot of times kids
will be super all prepared

00:09:54.317 --> 00:09:56.269
and then bomb in math test or the SAT

00:09:56.269 --> 00:09:57.859
or whatever it happens to be.

00:09:57.859 --> 00:09:59.064
And we look around and assume,

00:09:59.064 --> 00:10:01.493
well, you didn't study hard enough, right,

00:10:01.493 --> 00:10:02.841
or maybe you're just not that good,

00:10:02.841 --> 00:10:04.100
you're not as smart as
you thought you were.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.885
That's a thought that helps nobody, right?

00:10:06.885 --> 00:10:07.718
The tutor is no good.

00:10:07.718 --> 00:10:08.821
The program is no good.

00:10:08.821 --> 00:10:12.047
And those are all things
that we might wanna consider.

00:10:12.047 --> 00:10:15.287
But in some ways, if we
take seriously the fact

00:10:15.287 --> 00:10:19.120
that test anxiety is
rooted in neurochemistry,

00:10:19.978 --> 00:10:22.641
it doesn't, part of what
we worked to put hard

00:10:22.641 --> 00:10:25.108
and worked hard to put into kids' brains,

00:10:25.108 --> 00:10:27.654
that if under too much
pressure or perceived stress,

00:10:27.654 --> 00:10:28.627
they lose their minds,

00:10:28.627 --> 00:10:31.156
then it all goes for nothing.

00:10:31.156 --> 00:10:34.758
So I would say when things are unexpected

00:10:34.758 --> 00:10:37.173
is the way that I would
look for anxiety, right?

00:10:37.173 --> 00:10:40.998
If a kid is really, you know,
is taken this seriously,

00:10:40.998 --> 00:10:43.246
we have sense that they
demonstrate the abilities

00:10:43.246 --> 00:10:44.104
in other ways,

00:10:44.104 --> 00:10:46.333
and does class discussion
or essays or whatever,

00:10:46.333 --> 00:10:48.538
how do they perform when
there isn't pressure

00:10:48.538 --> 00:10:50.431
versus how they perform
when there is pressure.

00:10:50.431 --> 00:10:52.001
And if there's a big disconnect there.

00:10:52.001 --> 00:10:55.016
Then we start thinking about
how do we get more comfortable

00:10:55.016 --> 00:10:56.768
with that perceived pressure

00:10:56.768 --> 00:10:59.642
or find ways to adapt
to kind of ratchet down

00:10:59.642 --> 00:11:03.147
the perceived pressure of that activity.

00:11:03.147 --> 00:11:03.980
- That makes sense.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:06.781
Yeah, thinking about that
unexpected performance

00:11:06.781 --> 00:11:09.984
that doesn't align with what you see

00:11:09.984 --> 00:11:12.451
and other kinds of settings
and other kinds of ways

00:11:12.451 --> 00:11:14.709
that they've demonstrated what they now.

00:11:14.709 --> 00:11:17.744
All right, so how do we
help them ratchet that down?

00:11:17.744 --> 00:11:19.822
What does that look like?

00:11:19.822 --> 00:11:21.811
- Well, there's one place to start.

00:11:21.811 --> 00:11:25.433
There's wonderful research
by a woman named Sonia Lupien

00:11:25.433 --> 00:11:28.235
who said, it's the Centre
for Studies of Human Stress.

00:11:28.235 --> 00:11:30.599
I think I got that right
in Montreal, Canada.

00:11:30.599 --> 00:11:32.755
And she has a beautiful acronym

00:11:32.755 --> 00:11:35.346
of what makes people nuts, okay?

00:11:35.346 --> 00:11:36.747
So N.U.T.S, N-U-T-S.

00:11:36.747 --> 00:11:38.834
So N is novelty, so new situation.

00:11:38.834 --> 00:11:40.243
So, hey, it's not just the coronavirus

00:11:40.243 --> 00:11:42.931
'cause it's the novel coronavirus, right?

00:11:42.931 --> 00:11:44.178
If you're taking a test and you're like,

00:11:44.178 --> 00:11:45.216
I've never seen that before,

00:11:45.216 --> 00:11:47.710
it's never a happy thing
like if you're shopping.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:49.310
You as unpredictability,

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.889
so for the work that I
do in standardized tests,

00:11:51.889 --> 00:11:53.385
you know, am I gonna
have enough time or not,

00:11:53.385 --> 00:11:56.002
if you know that you won't
have time to do three questions

00:11:56.002 --> 00:11:57.167
no matter what you do,

00:11:57.167 --> 00:11:58.505
well, that's great to know.

00:11:58.505 --> 00:11:59.338
Then we figure out

00:11:59.338 --> 00:12:01.682
which three questions do
we just not do, right?

00:12:01.682 --> 00:12:03.402
We just fill in those answers.

00:12:03.402 --> 00:12:05.853
T is perceived threat.

00:12:05.853 --> 00:12:08.172
And so, pre-COVID for most kids,

00:12:08.172 --> 00:12:10.938
and for most of us in
particularly for kids in school,

00:12:10.938 --> 00:12:12.268
that's threat to ego.

00:12:12.268 --> 00:12:13.401
Am I gonna look stupid?

00:12:13.401 --> 00:12:14.692
Or people that think I'm an idiot.

00:12:14.692 --> 00:12:16.154
Do am I gonna think of my parents,

00:12:16.154 --> 00:12:18.129
gonna think of my teacher's gonna think.

00:12:18.129 --> 00:12:21.041
And then S is a low sense of control.

00:12:21.041 --> 00:12:23.884
And it turns out this is what
the book of Will and I wrote

00:12:23.884 --> 00:12:26.472
in "The Self-Driven Child" is
that a low sense of control

00:12:26.472 --> 00:12:30.129
is the most stressful thing
that people can experience.

00:12:30.129 --> 00:12:31.822
And so novelty, unpredictably,
the three, you know,

00:12:31.822 --> 00:12:34.641
this is one of the great
things about the Khan Academy

00:12:34.641 --> 00:12:37.049
is you can have a great teacher, awesome,

00:12:37.049 --> 00:12:39.060
and never need to spend
your time with Khan,

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.232
or you could have a teacher,

00:12:40.232 --> 00:12:42.258
you've had three teachers
in chemistry this year

00:12:42.258 --> 00:12:44.234
because, well, COVID, right?

00:12:44.234 --> 00:12:45.905
And you could feel like I'm responsible

00:12:45.905 --> 00:12:46.738
for doing well in this,

00:12:46.738 --> 00:12:48.210
but I have no way to learn this.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.749
Oh, oh, that's really stressful.

00:12:50.749 --> 00:12:51.936
If you know that there's a tool,

00:12:51.936 --> 00:12:54.207
it's not, gosh, you'd rather learn this

00:12:54.207 --> 00:12:56.217
at 10 o'clock in the morning
with a wonderful teacher,

00:12:56.217 --> 00:12:59.367
but that's not where we are right now.

00:12:59.367 --> 00:13:02.606
So if we think about in a given situation,

00:13:02.606 --> 00:13:04.759
how much of it is the novelty?

00:13:04.759 --> 00:13:06.452
How much is it that you feel threatened?

00:13:06.452 --> 00:13:08.708
How much of it that low sense of control?

00:13:08.708 --> 00:13:11.172
And we tack those things in turn.

00:13:11.172 --> 00:13:12.908
I can talk very credibly

00:13:12.908 --> 00:13:16.664
about the values that you
can't find a standardized test,

00:13:16.664 --> 00:13:19.228
but I can also talk until
I'm blue in the face

00:13:19.228 --> 00:13:21.927
about all the ways that
the SAT is a terrible test

00:13:21.927 --> 00:13:23.686
and they're a bunch of ragamuffins.

00:13:23.686 --> 00:13:25.734
And I do that with kids

00:13:25.734 --> 00:13:27.968
to decrease the stress
that they feel about,

00:13:27.968 --> 00:13:29.815
say, look, you may need to take this test

00:13:29.815 --> 00:13:31.607
for the college that you're applying to,

00:13:31.607 --> 00:13:34.017
but let's disabuse ourselves of the idea

00:13:34.017 --> 00:13:35.675
that this is measuring something innate,

00:13:35.675 --> 00:13:37.397
like your intelligence,

00:13:37.397 --> 00:13:39.256
because, golly, for most kids,

00:13:39.256 --> 00:13:41.486
that makes them super, super anxious.

00:13:41.486 --> 00:13:43.008
So that's kind of the first thing

00:13:43.008 --> 00:13:47.741
is we wanna try to diagnose
what's going on, right?

00:13:47.741 --> 00:13:48.880
I mean, a lot of kids I work with,

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:50.354
they have an older sibling, right?

00:13:50.354 --> 00:13:52.993
Sorry, sorry, your sister's
an incredible person,

00:13:52.993 --> 00:13:56.148
but she did you no favors
by getting a 1590 on the SAT

00:13:56.148 --> 00:13:58.132
and rolling off tonight at school.

00:13:58.132 --> 00:14:00.043
That's kind of intense, right?

00:14:00.043 --> 00:14:02.776
And so, this is something
that parents can do

00:14:02.776 --> 00:14:04.724
that the teachers can do.

00:14:04.724 --> 00:14:07.908
And you try to figure out
where, Tina Payne Bryson,

00:14:07.908 --> 00:14:11.940
if you know coauthor of
"The Whole-Brain child"

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:13.812
and a lot of other great books,

00:14:13.812 --> 00:14:15.778
talks about stress is what we perceive

00:14:15.778 --> 00:14:19.689
when there's a gap between the perceived,

00:14:19.689 --> 00:14:21.206
what's being required of us

00:14:21.206 --> 00:14:22.920
and what we think we're able to do.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:25.731
And so we may wanna decrease
the demands on a person

00:14:25.731 --> 00:14:27.829
or increase the support.

00:14:27.829 --> 00:14:29.771
Now, there's some really fun research,

00:14:29.771 --> 00:14:31.521
and it's just hard for parents there,

00:14:31.521 --> 00:14:32.538
when they ask parents

00:14:32.538 --> 00:14:35.875
kind of what their
expectations are of their kids?

00:14:35.875 --> 00:14:37.435
And then they also ask them, you know,

00:14:37.435 --> 00:14:38.268
and how much do you think

00:14:38.268 --> 00:14:40.635
they can actually meet those expectations?

00:14:40.635 --> 00:14:44.198
The wider the gap the worst things get.

00:14:44.198 --> 00:14:46.478
And I just spent a lot
of time with parents

00:14:46.478 --> 00:14:48.666
trying to talk them down, for now,

00:14:48.666 --> 00:14:51.050
these expectations that you
have to go to Princeton,

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:52.627
I love orange, but really
four years of orange.

00:14:52.627 --> 00:14:55.796
I mean, it's not the only
place in the world to go,

00:14:55.796 --> 00:14:57.336
but it's complicated.

00:14:57.336 --> 00:14:58.265
Kids are complicated.

00:14:58.265 --> 00:15:00.244
Parenting is complicated.

00:15:00.244 --> 00:15:05.000
But that will be the first
place that I would start.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:07.065
I mean, we talk about a lot.

00:15:07.065 --> 00:15:08.694
I spent the amount of
money that I've been paid

00:15:08.694 --> 00:15:10.614
to talk with kids about sleep,

00:15:10.614 --> 00:15:12.381
that I have not been paid to be talking

00:15:12.381 --> 00:15:13.374
about sleep deprivation.

00:15:13.374 --> 00:15:14.885
I've been paid to help them, you know,

00:15:14.885 --> 00:15:16.146
prepare for these tests.

00:15:16.146 --> 00:15:18.737
But I can talk for hours

00:15:18.737 --> 00:15:22.404
about what sleep
deprivation does to brains,

00:15:23.399 --> 00:15:24.463
one, to developing brains,

00:15:24.463 --> 00:15:25.754
but two, from a performance,

00:15:25.754 --> 00:15:27.349
just for folks who don't know.

00:15:27.349 --> 00:15:28.732
I mentioned a little while ago,

00:15:28.732 --> 00:15:31.658
cortisol was the dominant stress hormone.

00:15:31.658 --> 00:15:33.744
When we're sleep deprived,

00:15:33.744 --> 00:15:35.568
the amygdala, the stress reactions,

00:15:35.568 --> 00:15:37.064
about 60% more reactive,

00:15:37.064 --> 00:15:40.059
and our body's just pumping out cortisol.

00:15:40.059 --> 00:15:41.941
And so we're stressed all the time.

00:15:41.941 --> 00:15:44.067
There's a kid I was
working with years ago,

00:15:44.067 --> 00:15:45.016
I met her in Princeton,

00:15:45.016 --> 00:15:46.569
she had an older brother
who went to Princeton

00:15:46.569 --> 00:15:47.803
who was a baseball player.

00:15:47.803 --> 00:15:49.099
She was super academic,

00:15:49.099 --> 00:15:50.635
but found test harder part

00:15:50.635 --> 00:15:52.906
because she was just
more stressed more easily

00:15:52.906 --> 00:15:55.161
than, you know, maybe her brother was.

00:15:55.161 --> 00:15:56.180
So we're doing the ACT

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:59.652
and she has taken this
test far too many times.

00:15:59.652 --> 00:16:01.184
And we're meeting on a Sunday,

00:16:01.184 --> 00:16:03.864
she's taking a test
the following Saturday.

00:16:03.864 --> 00:16:05.201
We're doing all the last room interview

00:16:05.201 --> 00:16:08.214
I think she's an academic
kid, great family.

00:16:08.214 --> 00:16:10.327
And I finally say, and reminder that,

00:16:10.327 --> 00:16:12.576
if you can be in bed by 10 or 10:30,

00:16:12.576 --> 00:16:14.279
you're gonna do so much
better on the test,

00:16:14.279 --> 00:16:16.927
well, then I'm in the middle
of this and her face hauls.

00:16:16.927 --> 00:16:18.677
And I'm like, "What?"

00:16:19.572 --> 00:16:21.599
And she said, "Well, my friends."

00:16:21.599 --> 00:16:22.628
And her friends were great

00:16:22.628 --> 00:16:23.749
and they're super important to her.

00:16:23.749 --> 00:16:25.360
She's a 17 year old girl, I get that.

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:27.134
I said, "I understand that, I understand."

00:16:27.134 --> 00:16:29.630
"But for one week, could we
maybe just like, you know,

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:30.714
just hanging up with
the phone in the kitchen

00:16:30.714 --> 00:16:32.728
to go to bed early?"

00:16:32.728 --> 00:16:33.561
"Maybe."

00:16:33.561 --> 00:16:36.768
"So what if your mom
paid you to go to bed?"

00:16:36.768 --> 00:16:38.421
She's like, "My mom wouldn't pay me."

00:16:38.421 --> 00:16:41.006
I'm like, "what if I paid you?"

00:16:41.006 --> 00:16:42.443
She's like, "what?"

00:16:42.443 --> 00:16:44.218
And I said, "But I don't trust you.

00:16:44.218 --> 00:16:45.051
So your mom's in charge.

00:16:45.051 --> 00:16:46.932
You have to hand your phone
to your mama at 10 o'clock.

00:16:46.932 --> 00:16:48.217
And if you're in bed and lights out,

00:16:48.217 --> 00:16:49.666
I'll give you 20 bucks."

00:16:49.666 --> 00:16:52.897
And she said, "For the week?"

00:16:52.897 --> 00:16:55.269
I said, "I'll give you $20 every night."

00:16:55.269 --> 00:16:56.359
She said, "No, you won't."

00:16:56.359 --> 00:16:57.192
I said, "Yes, I will."

00:16:57.192 --> 00:16:59.872
She said, "No," I said, "Watch me."

00:16:59.872 --> 00:17:02.001
She shows up on Friday, we
have one last more to do,

00:17:02.001 --> 00:17:04.006
and I hand her five $20 bills.

00:17:04.006 --> 00:17:05.511
Now this is wealth redistribution

00:17:05.511 --> 00:17:07.195
is really a parent's money.

00:17:07.195 --> 00:17:08.848
And she goes off and gets the test.

00:17:08.848 --> 00:17:10.612
I mean, and she had always been capable

00:17:10.612 --> 00:17:13.220
that she could have probably
done this months ago

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:16.316
if she could have been less stressed.

00:17:16.316 --> 00:17:19.101
So sleep deprivation is a big deal.

00:17:19.101 --> 00:17:19.934
There's a wonderful book,

00:17:19.934 --> 00:17:21.286
I'll hold this up called "Choke."

00:17:21.286 --> 00:17:23.518
It's the provocative
title if you know this.

00:17:23.518 --> 00:17:24.465
This is Sian Beilock.

00:17:24.465 --> 00:17:26.132
She's now the president
of Barnard College.

00:17:26.132 --> 00:17:28.338
She is incredible.

00:17:28.338 --> 00:17:29.517
Love, love, love her work.

00:17:29.517 --> 00:17:32.771
And she looks at
underperformance public speaking,

00:17:32.771 --> 00:17:34.599
athletic standardized tests.

00:17:34.599 --> 00:17:37.263
And one of her models is the idea

00:17:37.263 --> 00:17:40.180
of having the mindset of a predator

00:17:41.453 --> 00:17:43.048
rather than a prey,

00:17:43.048 --> 00:17:44.657
because the prefrontal cortex,

00:17:44.657 --> 00:17:46.011
decision-making, planning,

00:17:46.011 --> 00:17:49.156
problem solving, and
emotional mental flexibility,

00:17:49.156 --> 00:17:51.562
all executive functions
of the prefrontal cortex,

00:17:51.562 --> 00:17:53.929
the amygdala, the freeze,
fight, or flight response.

00:17:53.929 --> 00:17:55.477
You don't wanna freeze,
you can't run away,

00:17:55.477 --> 00:17:58.458
and there's nobody to invite
right in the middle of a test.

00:17:58.458 --> 00:18:01.495
So we really don't want that
part of our brain going off.

00:18:01.495 --> 00:18:03.644
And she says, "If you lean in with,"

00:18:03.644 --> 00:18:06.876
what she describes, "this is
kind of having more swagger,

00:18:06.876 --> 00:18:08.115
it can help you perform better."

00:18:08.115 --> 00:18:09.636
So I actually did it.

00:18:09.636 --> 00:18:11.186
I did a TikTok thing in the day to shoot

00:18:11.186 --> 00:18:13.691
and now I know, old people
on TikTok, but whatever.

00:18:13.691 --> 00:18:16.584
And so, I stumbled on
this great research at NIH

00:18:16.584 --> 00:18:18.931
that found this of executive functions,

00:18:18.931 --> 00:18:23.026
we know that stress really
messes with executive functions,

00:18:23.026 --> 00:18:24.708
being anxious, bad, bad, bad,

00:18:24.708 --> 00:18:26.965
but you know what doesn't?

00:18:26.965 --> 00:18:28.660
Being angry.

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:30.846
So if you're worried about the SAT

00:18:30.846 --> 00:18:32.024
or your math test, whatever,

00:18:32.024 --> 00:18:33.469
I feel like, oh, ragamuffins, I hate them.

00:18:33.469 --> 00:18:34.702
(speaking gibberish)

00:18:34.702 --> 00:18:36.797
You're better often mad angry.

00:18:36.797 --> 00:18:37.963
Then you are being anxious

00:18:37.963 --> 00:18:41.058
'cause you'll actually think better.

00:18:41.058 --> 00:18:42.647
So there's that.

00:18:42.647 --> 00:18:44.429
- So interesting.
- You have more questions?

00:18:44.429 --> 00:18:45.805
I have another one I'll
talk about in a minute,

00:18:45.805 --> 00:18:47.355
but please jump in.

00:18:47.355 --> 00:18:48.559
- Yeah, no, that was great.

00:18:48.559 --> 00:18:52.735
So you talked about
lowering that, you know,

00:18:52.735 --> 00:18:54.069
lowering the threshold.

00:18:54.069 --> 00:18:55.895
When we think about teachers,

00:18:55.895 --> 00:19:00.895
we have a question that came
in thinking about from Aviv,

00:19:01.326 --> 00:19:04.108
what are the actions teachers can take

00:19:04.108 --> 00:19:05.473
to help their students prepare

00:19:05.473 --> 00:19:07.340
for that stress of assessment?

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:08.849
So even if we're not talking SAT,

00:19:08.849 --> 00:19:11.720
we could be talking classroom assessments-

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:12.986
- That's the great part.
- like are there other things

00:19:12.986 --> 00:19:14.832
that teachers can do.

00:19:14.832 --> 00:19:15.665
- Yeah, yeah.

00:19:15.665 --> 00:19:17.375
I mean, there's several things.

00:19:17.375 --> 00:19:19.125
I mean, first of all,

00:19:20.096 --> 00:19:23.421
there's a wonderful
study that when students,

00:19:23.421 --> 00:19:25.227
I think they were taking
a GRE, I can't remember,

00:19:25.227 --> 00:19:27.424
but this of course applies to all tests,

00:19:27.424 --> 00:19:31.299
when kids were as test-takers reminded

00:19:31.299 --> 00:19:33.589
that some anxiety, back
to that bell curve,

00:19:33.589 --> 00:19:35.772
actually improves their
performance, right?

00:19:35.772 --> 00:19:37.418
That actually, you wanna have

00:19:37.418 --> 00:19:38.814
some butterflies before the test.

00:19:38.814 --> 00:19:40.739
That actually helps you focus.

00:19:40.739 --> 00:19:41.829
That improve their performance

00:19:41.829 --> 00:19:44.824
because then they weren't
worried about being worried.

00:19:44.824 --> 00:19:47.019
They felt what they're
feeling physiologically.

00:19:47.019 --> 00:19:51.448
They felt as this is me
preparing to go do battle, right?

00:19:51.448 --> 00:19:53.198
That's super helpful.

00:19:54.149 --> 00:19:55.677
The same Sonia Lupien of the N.U.T.S.,

00:19:55.677 --> 00:19:57.242
one of the things that she talks about,

00:19:57.242 --> 00:19:59.654
this incredibly useful tool

00:19:59.654 --> 00:20:02.759
that like probably the single
best tool to lower stress

00:20:02.759 --> 00:20:04.342
is plan B thinking.

00:20:05.823 --> 00:20:08.928
I am so happy for test optional,

00:20:08.928 --> 00:20:10.876
I can't even tell you,

00:20:10.876 --> 00:20:12.388
because it lowers the stress.

00:20:12.388 --> 00:20:14.618
You know, of course you
would love to get a 1500

00:20:14.618 --> 00:20:15.451
because who wouldn't?

00:20:15.451 --> 00:20:16.995
And I applied it to
Princeton, fantastic, right?

00:20:16.995 --> 00:20:18.958
'Cause you look great in orange, I'm sure.

00:20:18.958 --> 00:20:21.088
But goodness knows, you know,
if you don't get a 1500,

00:20:21.088 --> 00:20:22.256
well, know what should we do?

00:20:22.256 --> 00:20:24.042
You can still apply to
Princeton, you know?

00:20:24.042 --> 00:20:25.593
And you know what happens if
you don't go to Princeton?

00:20:25.593 --> 00:20:26.902
There are like 3,500 colleges

00:20:26.902 --> 00:20:28.447
where you can also get, you know.

00:20:28.447 --> 00:20:33.409
And so, in order to talk
about that plan B thinking,

00:20:33.409 --> 00:20:34.932
a teacher really needs to have

00:20:34.932 --> 00:20:36.843
a good relationship with the kid.

00:20:36.843 --> 00:20:39.120
Less the kid feel like
you're trying to say,

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:40.115
you saying I can't do this.

00:20:40.115 --> 00:20:41.368
Not what I'm saying at all.

00:20:41.368 --> 00:20:45.006
I'm saying that it will
help you if, right?

00:20:45.006 --> 00:20:47.118
From a stress perspective,

00:20:47.118 --> 00:20:48.976
Bill and I in "The Self-Driven Child,"

00:20:48.976 --> 00:20:51.682
we talk about the chapter in the book

00:20:51.682 --> 00:20:54.974
called the non-anxious presence.

00:20:54.974 --> 00:20:55.971
And I wish we'd made this up

00:20:55.971 --> 00:20:56.979
'cause it's such a good term.

00:20:56.979 --> 00:20:58.656
We borrowed this from a
guy named Edward Freeman

00:20:58.656 --> 00:20:59.809
who's a rabbi.

00:20:59.809 --> 00:21:00.642
He was a consultant.

00:21:00.642 --> 00:21:01.890
He worked with schools and searchers

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:03.328
and synagogues and families.

00:21:03.328 --> 00:21:07.314
And he simply made the point
that things work better

00:21:07.314 --> 00:21:10.261
when the person or people in
charge aren't overly emotional,

00:21:10.261 --> 00:21:11.342
aren't over-reactive.

00:21:11.342 --> 00:21:14.662
And because stress is contagious,

00:21:14.662 --> 00:21:16.302
but the good news is this,

00:21:16.302 --> 00:21:18.038
all emotions are contagious.

00:21:18.038 --> 00:21:18.906
Calm is contagious,

00:21:18.906 --> 00:21:21.561
we discovered this when calm is contagious

00:21:21.561 --> 00:21:23.957
is a mantra of the Navy seals.

00:21:23.957 --> 00:21:26.222
So if your kids come to you

00:21:26.222 --> 00:21:28.199
and they're super spun up, right?

00:21:28.199 --> 00:21:31.449
If you can say, you know, Eli Lebowitz,

00:21:33.066 --> 00:21:36.555
a professor of anxiety of
what's called the SPACE,

00:21:36.555 --> 00:21:37.681
but we'll go into it,

00:21:37.681 --> 00:21:40.772
and yeah, Eli talks about
making supportive statements.

00:21:40.772 --> 00:21:42.774
And supportive statements
are simply saying,

00:21:42.774 --> 00:21:45.665
yeah, I get that this
is really hard for you.

00:21:45.665 --> 00:21:48.574
I can see that you're pretty
stressed out about this.

00:21:48.574 --> 00:21:50.813
And I have every confidence
that you can deal with this.

00:21:50.813 --> 00:21:51.986
I'm confident you can handle this.

00:21:51.986 --> 00:21:53.993
And I'll help you in any way that I can.

00:21:53.993 --> 00:21:56.061
Because so often we try
to say, "Oh my gosh,

00:21:56.061 --> 00:21:57.214
it's not such a big deal, Kris,

00:21:57.214 --> 00:21:58.047
it's just, oh my gosh."

00:21:58.047 --> 00:22:00.167
And you're three hours
from that who even care?

00:22:00.167 --> 00:22:01.824
Well, I'm trying to help you,

00:22:01.824 --> 00:22:04.505
but I'm really invalidating
what you're feeling.

00:22:04.505 --> 00:22:07.028
And you're feeling what you're
feeling for whatever reason.

00:22:07.028 --> 00:22:07.861
But we also say,

00:22:07.861 --> 00:22:10.155
I have every confidence that
this is gonna work on it.

00:22:10.155 --> 00:22:12.673
If it just worked out just the
way you want, we can do this.

00:22:12.673 --> 00:22:13.729
And I bet you can do this.

00:22:13.729 --> 00:22:15.635
And you're gonna figure out
a way to make this work,

00:22:15.635 --> 00:22:17.747
even if it's a plan B.

00:22:17.747 --> 00:22:21.574
So in our new book "What Do You Say?"

00:22:21.574 --> 00:22:23.238
We have a lot of scripts,

00:22:23.238 --> 00:22:24.515
there are a lot of language

00:22:24.515 --> 00:22:27.272
because it's hard as a teacher

00:22:27.272 --> 00:22:28.711
when kids come at you with things

00:22:28.711 --> 00:22:30.837
and they're super spun up and
your head, you're thinking,

00:22:30.837 --> 00:22:33.378
oh my goodness, oh, wait,
wait, wait, time out.

00:22:33.378 --> 00:22:35.364
We could be seeing this differently.

00:22:35.364 --> 00:22:37.831
But what we're really trying
to do is get, thank you.

00:22:37.831 --> 00:22:39.061
Yeah, that's the book.

00:22:39.061 --> 00:22:40.269
Will Stixurd, genius.

00:22:40.269 --> 00:22:42.785
What we're really trying
to do is get buy-in

00:22:42.785 --> 00:22:46.237
in order to have kids
be open to our messages,

00:22:46.237 --> 00:22:49.054
we really just start with
empathy and validation.

00:22:49.054 --> 00:22:49.974
It makes sense to me

00:22:49.974 --> 00:22:51.453
that you're pretty stressed out about this

00:22:51.453 --> 00:22:53.878
as it feels like a big deal.

00:22:53.878 --> 00:22:54.940
It makes sense to me.

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:56.735
I can get that you're pretty,

00:22:56.735 --> 00:22:58.462
feel pretty intense about
this test having right now,

00:22:58.462 --> 00:23:00.825
because there's some hard stuff on that,

00:23:00.825 --> 00:23:02.950
but I'm confident that
you can handle this.

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:04.033
So, you know,

00:23:06.687 --> 00:23:08.145
I don't wanna put one more thing

00:23:08.145 --> 00:23:09.921
on the backs of teachers who are,

00:23:09.921 --> 00:23:10.854
it feels like they're shouldering

00:23:10.854 --> 00:23:12.754
the weight of the world right now.

00:23:12.754 --> 00:23:17.081
But I will say that if
there's any single thing

00:23:17.081 --> 00:23:20.671
that is valuable to
young people right now,

00:23:20.671 --> 00:23:23.831
it's having a close connection
to one or more adults.

00:23:23.831 --> 00:23:25.319
If there's a silver bullet

00:23:25.319 --> 00:23:28.493
against the effects of
stress and developing brains,

00:23:28.493 --> 00:23:31.162
it's feeling close connection to an adult,

00:23:31.162 --> 00:23:32.475
ideally that's parents.

00:23:32.475 --> 00:23:33.733
When we were writing this book,

00:23:33.733 --> 00:23:36.410
we were meeting with all
these kids and we asked them,

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:38.642
who do you feel closest to in the world?

00:23:38.642 --> 00:23:40.177
Sometimes with mom, sometimes with dad,

00:23:40.177 --> 00:23:41.733
but sometime with grandma,
my aunt, my uncle,

00:23:41.733 --> 00:23:43.019
sometimes the teacher.

00:23:43.019 --> 00:23:45.245
I'm sitting in right now in Washington DC

00:23:45.245 --> 00:23:47.266
and right across the street
from us is Wilson High School,

00:23:47.266 --> 00:23:49.765
it's kind of the big public
high school in Northwest,

00:23:49.765 --> 00:23:52.198
terrific school, great learners.

00:23:52.198 --> 00:23:53.031
And we're working with,

00:23:53.031 --> 00:23:55.344
we were talking with a bunch
of this kids, and this one,

00:23:55.344 --> 00:23:57.626
and so, in the follow-up
question, we said,

00:23:57.626 --> 00:24:00.521
"What about those people
makes you feel close to them?"

00:24:00.521 --> 00:24:01.354
And there are two things.

00:24:01.354 --> 00:24:05.148
One, they listened to me without judging.

00:24:05.148 --> 00:24:08.212
And two, they don't tell
me what to do all the time.

00:24:08.212 --> 00:24:11.337
So this boy went on to talk
about this biology teacher

00:24:11.337 --> 00:24:15.850
who was just like this
fountain of wisdom and calm.

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.764
She wasn't his teacher,
she wasn't his teacher.

00:24:18.764 --> 00:24:20.274
She'd never had him as a teacher.

00:24:20.274 --> 00:24:22.248
He never had her as a teacher, excuse me.

00:24:22.248 --> 00:24:24.122
But she was just known as the person

00:24:24.122 --> 00:24:26.087
and all these kids
would just come in there

00:24:26.087 --> 00:24:27.947
and they would just have lunch with her.

00:24:27.947 --> 00:24:29.424
And she just asked them about their stuff.

00:24:29.424 --> 00:24:32.622
And I'm sure they told
her some crazy stuff.

00:24:32.622 --> 00:24:34.830
And, Lily, just being a
person where you can go,

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:38.461
"Oh, that's interesting
tell me more about that."

00:24:38.461 --> 00:24:41.562
Because it's incredibly
valuable for lowering stress

00:24:41.562 --> 00:24:44.422
and for developing motivation.

00:24:44.422 --> 00:24:46.059
We can talk about motivation if you want,

00:24:46.059 --> 00:24:48.132
but I'm an enormous fan of what's called,

00:24:48.132 --> 00:24:48.982
we are known as fans,

00:24:48.982 --> 00:24:51.107
of what's known as
self-determination theory.

00:24:51.107 --> 00:24:53.355
And it's a model for intrinsic motivation,

00:24:53.355 --> 00:24:55.495
you probably, I know
you know all about this.

00:24:55.495 --> 00:24:58.345
- Yes, go ahead and explain
it I'm a big fan too.

00:24:58.345 --> 00:24:59.670
- But the idea that, you know,

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:00.745
so often we think, you know,

00:25:00.745 --> 00:25:02.682
parents, teachers, school
systems, politicians,

00:25:02.682 --> 00:25:04.585
"We've got to get kids to work hard."

00:25:04.585 --> 00:25:05.842
Well, from my perspective,

00:25:05.842 --> 00:25:08.197
I have zero interest in kids working hard.

00:25:08.197 --> 00:25:11.796
I have interest in they're
wanting to work hard

00:25:11.796 --> 00:25:15.698
because extrinsic motivation
of fear or bribes,

00:25:15.698 --> 00:25:17.345
they work for a while,

00:25:17.345 --> 00:25:20.357
but it's a terrible way
to grow up with a brain,

00:25:20.357 --> 00:25:23.119
simply trying to meet other
people's expectations of you.

00:25:23.119 --> 00:25:26.769
What we really want is
kids to do the work, right?

00:25:26.769 --> 00:25:29.007
Jess Lahey, you know "The Gift of Failure"

00:25:29.007 --> 00:25:30.755
and people I know, love her work.

00:25:30.755 --> 00:25:33.745
I saw how she talked with a
bunch of teachers and ask them,

00:25:33.745 --> 00:25:34.578
and this one teacher said,

00:25:34.578 --> 00:25:37.814
"You know what the three
R's of teaching are?

00:25:37.814 --> 00:25:41.103
Relationships, relationships,
relationships."

00:25:41.103 --> 00:25:44.989
And so, good teachers are
worth their week in gold

00:25:44.989 --> 00:25:47.002
'cause they don't get kids to work hard.

00:25:47.002 --> 00:25:48.675
They get kids to want to work hard.

00:25:48.675 --> 00:25:49.974
Like whenever I meet with kids,

00:25:49.974 --> 00:25:52.248
the first day is trying to
understand them as a learner,

00:25:52.248 --> 00:25:53.223
like what's your favorite class?

00:25:53.223 --> 00:25:54.087
Which your least favorite class?

00:25:54.087 --> 00:25:54.998
Oh, you love math.

00:25:54.998 --> 00:25:56.987
Is that the class, or is that the teacher?

00:25:56.987 --> 00:25:58.959
And as you can imagine,

00:25:58.959 --> 00:26:00.006
more than half the time it's,

00:26:00.006 --> 00:26:01.355
"Oh, you know, it's pretty good,

00:26:01.355 --> 00:26:03.139
but, oh, Ms. Rodriguez,
she's just, I don't know,

00:26:03.139 --> 00:26:05.613
she makes it so interesting."

00:26:05.613 --> 00:26:08.416
And part of that is that she
makes the math interesting,

00:26:08.416 --> 00:26:11.833
but that she's interested in them, right?

00:26:13.464 --> 00:26:16.131
And so, again, I just, you know,

00:26:17.889 --> 00:26:19.700
I wish teachers have more autonomy.

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:22.776
I wish they had less on their
plates so that they just,

00:26:22.776 --> 00:26:25.163
it was easier for them to
put as much time and energy

00:26:25.163 --> 00:26:27.565
as they wanted to there
in the relationships

00:26:27.565 --> 00:26:28.916
'cause for anyone who's an old

00:26:28.916 --> 00:26:30.703
for like when you think back

00:26:30.703 --> 00:26:32.143
about the class that you love most,

00:26:32.143 --> 00:26:33.006
I can remember,

00:26:33.006 --> 00:26:35.477
yeah, you can remember so
little of what we learned,

00:26:35.477 --> 00:26:37.489
but I can remember Mr. Roberman

00:26:37.489 --> 00:26:39.378
and just how much I love this
guy as a history teacher,

00:26:39.378 --> 00:26:41.886
I took zero history in college,

00:26:41.886 --> 00:26:43.936
but I loved him as a teacher, right?

00:26:43.936 --> 00:26:46.824
And that's what great teachers do, right?

00:26:46.824 --> 00:26:48.514
- Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:26:48.514 --> 00:26:52.887
Let's take one more question
from YouTube the handle,

00:26:52.887 --> 00:26:54.461
Math With TJA says,

00:26:54.461 --> 00:26:57.635
"How do I clear my mind
after a big failure?"

00:26:57.635 --> 00:27:00.146
So you can imagine you failed at something

00:27:00.146 --> 00:27:01.698
or that's causes a lot of stress.

00:27:01.698 --> 00:27:04.861
How do you get over that, and go for that,

00:27:04.861 --> 00:27:07.761
you know, what the next
steps are gonna be?

00:27:07.761 --> 00:27:11.094
- Well, gosh, I mean, one thing there's,

00:27:12.614 --> 00:27:15.153
Lisa DiMora, if you know, I
love all these people and she's.

00:27:15.153 --> 00:27:17.728
- She's a previous Ed Talk's guest.

00:27:17.728 --> 00:27:19.951
- Oh, I just love her, you know.

00:27:19.951 --> 00:27:23.664
And she makes the point that mental health

00:27:23.664 --> 00:27:26.447
is the right feeling
for the right situation.

00:27:26.447 --> 00:27:28.861
So if you really worked hard at a test

00:27:28.861 --> 00:27:30.898
and you feel like you bombed it,

00:27:30.898 --> 00:27:32.948
you're allowed to be upset, right?

00:27:32.948 --> 00:27:34.211
You shouldn't be saying,
I shouldn't be upset.

00:27:34.211 --> 00:27:35.474
Look, you're allowed to be pissed.

00:27:35.474 --> 00:27:38.824
Like, gosh, darn it, I
really worked hard in that.

00:27:38.824 --> 00:27:42.026
But we then have to move on, right?

00:27:42.026 --> 00:27:43.138
What the real challenge

00:27:43.138 --> 00:27:44.813
is when people get locked in rumination,

00:27:44.813 --> 00:27:46.390
they beat themselves
and beat themselves up,

00:27:46.390 --> 00:27:48.475
and beat themselves up,
and beat themselves up.

00:27:48.475 --> 00:27:50.084
And what we wanna try to do is

00:27:50.084 --> 00:27:51.664
if you go back to that plan B thinking,

00:27:51.664 --> 00:27:53.511
what else can I do?

00:27:53.511 --> 00:27:55.597
Now the reality is, I love this,

00:27:55.597 --> 00:27:57.472
this is a success coach
I follow in the 80s

00:27:57.472 --> 00:28:00.244
who made the point that unexpected success

00:28:00.244 --> 00:28:02.268
and unexpected failures,

00:28:02.268 --> 00:28:04.779
those are what provide the source material

00:28:04.779 --> 00:28:06.854
for great insights on your own life.

00:28:06.854 --> 00:28:09.950
So it's worth trying what
happened there, right?

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:12.678
So talk to your math teacher,
talk to your friends,

00:28:12.678 --> 00:28:14.256
compare notes, reflect.

00:28:14.256 --> 00:28:16.646
Was I really well-prepared?

00:28:16.646 --> 00:28:18.072
Was I well rested?

00:28:18.072 --> 00:28:18.968
What was going on?

00:28:18.968 --> 00:28:20.393
What was my subjective state?

00:28:20.393 --> 00:28:22.637
What did it feel like going in there?

00:28:22.637 --> 00:28:26.240
I mean, I feel that the
kids every year in school,

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:28.680
and I don't know what
grade the student is in,

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:30.193
we should have to work hard

00:28:30.193 --> 00:28:32.319
and we shouldn't reengineer
ourselves learning,

00:28:32.319 --> 00:28:33.747
because if you're not having to do things

00:28:33.747 --> 00:28:35.372
more or differently,

00:28:35.372 --> 00:28:36.957
in 11th grade that you did in 7th grade,

00:28:36.957 --> 00:28:38.435
like, boy, a lot of people's time

00:28:38.435 --> 00:28:41.319
is being wasted here, right?

00:28:41.319 --> 00:28:43.730
There's a wonderful, wonderful,
wonderful book called,

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:45.517
"Make It Stick."

00:28:45.517 --> 00:28:48.780
It's Henry Roediger the
third and a bunch of people.

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:50.991
If there's a, if you send
them notes afterwards,

00:28:50.991 --> 00:28:52.992
he has, they have, they, I
forget the other authors.

00:28:52.992 --> 00:28:55.477
I just know his name,
Michael Brown I think,

00:28:55.477 --> 00:28:56.753
David Brown, something like that.

00:28:56.753 --> 00:29:00.965
They have a fantastic
piece in psychology today

00:29:00.965 --> 00:29:04.902
with basically the six
takeaways from "Make It Stick."

00:29:04.902 --> 00:29:06.435
And it's things that you
have know all the stuff,

00:29:06.435 --> 00:29:07.873
but it's spaced learning.

00:29:07.873 --> 00:29:09.060
It's sleep.

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:11.720
It's the testing effect, right?

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:12.983
So retrieval practice.

00:29:12.983 --> 00:29:14.247
So we don't just look at our notes,

00:29:14.247 --> 00:29:16.033
we actually kind of test ourselves.

00:29:16.033 --> 00:29:17.822
There's a lot of great stuff there.

00:29:17.822 --> 00:29:19.037
And so it's worth,

00:29:19.037 --> 00:29:21.777
when I think about
underperformance on tests,

00:29:21.777 --> 00:29:24.424
math tests, SAT and that kind of thing.

00:29:24.424 --> 00:29:25.257
I try to think about,

00:29:25.257 --> 00:29:28.160
was it the input or was it the output?

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:30.784
Meaning did I test myself in ways

00:29:30.784 --> 00:29:33.231
that I know that I really learned it?

00:29:33.231 --> 00:29:35.714
And if I, because if I
just think I knew it,

00:29:35.714 --> 00:29:38.439
it might've been a little trick
my brain was playing on me,

00:29:38.439 --> 00:29:41.365
in which case I wanna examine
how it is that I'm learning?

00:29:41.365 --> 00:29:42.636
Was I just cramming it?

00:29:42.636 --> 00:29:45.476
But if I really knew the stuff
and I ended up performing,

00:29:45.476 --> 00:29:46.990
well, then it's a
performance piece, right?

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:49.464
So we're back to, you know,
what blows up cortisol?

00:29:49.464 --> 00:29:52.829
What helps you get your swagger on, right?

00:29:52.829 --> 00:29:54.967
What gets you like Hulk smash mode?

00:29:54.967 --> 00:29:56.392
When we take the test, right?

00:29:56.392 --> 00:29:58.482
Listening to music.

00:29:58.482 --> 00:30:01.109
I'm a huge, the number of students

00:30:01.109 --> 00:30:05.359
to whom I have prescribed
the seven-minute workout.

00:30:06.448 --> 00:30:08.461
Sonia, people don't know this,

00:30:08.461 --> 00:30:11.722
it's 7, it's 12, 30-second exercise

00:30:11.722 --> 00:30:12.956
that you can do anywhere you need,

00:30:12.956 --> 00:30:16.443
no exercise equipment at
all, you need a chair.

00:30:16.443 --> 00:30:18.540
Because, oh, if we go back
to Sonia Lupien, yeah.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:20.364
I love her, she's just a genius.

00:30:20.364 --> 00:30:24.110
She says, "The four methods
of emergency stress relief

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:27.860
are these, laughing,
singing, deep breathing,

00:30:28.772 --> 00:30:30.550
and vigorous exercise" right?

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:31.613
Well, laughing and singing probably

00:30:31.613 --> 00:30:33.940
aren't too available too during the test,

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:36.202
maybe beforehand, vigorous exercise,

00:30:36.202 --> 00:30:37.303
for sure you can do this.

00:30:37.303 --> 00:30:40.490
In Paul Tuff's book, he has
a whole chapter about me.

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:41.617
I won't go into long story,

00:30:41.617 --> 00:30:44.926
but basically showing up in this girl's,

00:30:44.926 --> 00:30:48.063
going to her house and
doing SoulCycle with them.

00:30:48.063 --> 00:30:49.726
It's a wack-a-doodle story

00:30:49.726 --> 00:30:51.976
at like 6:30 in the morning of an ACT.

00:30:51.976 --> 00:30:53.059
It's bonkers.

00:30:53.923 --> 00:30:55.264
And to blow off the cortisol,

00:30:55.264 --> 00:30:57.964
super academic, super time mnemonic.

00:30:57.964 --> 00:30:59.306
I couldn't get her sleep more.

00:30:59.306 --> 00:31:01.930
And just, she sort of gassed
herself for 20 minutes,

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:02.763
took a shower.

00:31:02.763 --> 00:31:05.916
I went away and got the score

00:31:05.916 --> 00:31:07.163
because too much cortisol.

00:31:07.163 --> 00:31:10.222
She was just on the wrong
side of that bell curve.

00:31:10.222 --> 00:31:12.512
And then the deep
breathing is a big thing.

00:31:12.512 --> 00:31:14.275
People probably know the box breathing

00:31:14.275 --> 00:31:16.195
that the Navy seals created.

00:31:16.195 --> 00:31:18.032
I mean, these are people
who have, you know,

00:31:18.032 --> 00:31:21.629
probably high intensity
performance anxiety, right?

00:31:21.629 --> 00:31:23.837
And deep breathing

00:31:23.837 --> 00:31:25.675
purges cortisol out of our nervous system

00:31:25.675 --> 00:31:26.965
and brings it back to normal.

00:31:26.965 --> 00:31:29.789
I mean, pre-COVID, I was
still taking the SAT,

00:31:29.789 --> 00:31:31.862
shoulder to shoulder with teenagers.

00:31:31.862 --> 00:31:33.341
And, you know, just to
know what it feels like,

00:31:33.341 --> 00:31:36.760
and I can sit there if my
pulse is still going like crazy

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:38.707
because I'm an old guy in
a room full of teenagers,

00:31:38.707 --> 00:31:39.817
like that's weird, right?

00:31:39.817 --> 00:31:41.537
But I wanna know what it feels like.

00:31:41.537 --> 00:31:43.514
And I sit there when I'm
filling in my address,

00:31:43.514 --> 00:31:46.677
I do deep breathing and
it brings me back down.

00:31:46.677 --> 00:31:48.404
I'm not all tranquillo,

00:31:48.404 --> 00:31:52.477
but I'm in this place where
I'm amped up but not crazy.

00:31:52.477 --> 00:31:54.378
So again, I think of input in testing,

00:31:54.378 --> 00:31:55.589
do they really know the stuff?

00:31:55.589 --> 00:31:57.563
Look up at "Make It Stick" it's wonderful.

00:31:57.563 --> 00:31:59.551
But then if you really know
that you learned it well,

00:31:59.551 --> 00:32:03.206
then we start thinking about
these aspects of performance

00:32:03.206 --> 00:32:06.819
and it may be a little bit
of A and a little bit of B.

00:32:06.819 --> 00:32:08.476
- Great, great.

00:32:08.476 --> 00:32:10.040
We are just about out of time.

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:13.077
I do want you, as you
say, in your new book,

00:32:13.077 --> 00:32:15.893
you provide lots of
scripts and things to ask.

00:32:15.893 --> 00:32:18.168
Let me ask you this
question that comes up a lot

00:32:18.168 --> 00:32:19.904
when talking to parents,

00:32:19.904 --> 00:32:22.466
how do I ask something
that gets my kids to say,

00:32:22.466 --> 00:32:24.289
give me an answer other than fine.

00:32:24.289 --> 00:32:26.789
When I say, "How are you doing?"

00:32:26.789 --> 00:32:27.624
"Fine."

00:32:27.624 --> 00:32:28.859
"How was school today?"

00:32:28.859 --> 00:32:29.869
"Fine."

00:32:29.869 --> 00:32:34.217
How can we get kids to
express their emotions?

00:32:34.217 --> 00:32:36.929
- Yeah, I mean, oh, golly.

00:32:36.929 --> 00:32:38.027
I mean, first of all,

00:32:38.027 --> 00:32:40.587
it's probably helped for
us to remind ourselves

00:32:40.587 --> 00:32:42.362
that teenagers, especially,

00:32:42.362 --> 00:32:44.086
part of their work to become adults

00:32:44.086 --> 00:32:46.988
is to individuate from mom and dad, right?

00:32:46.988 --> 00:32:49.353
So they're supposed to push away from us

00:32:49.353 --> 00:32:50.275
a little bit, right?

00:32:50.275 --> 00:32:51.963
And so, they run away
and then they come back,

00:32:51.963 --> 00:32:52.974
and run away, come back.

00:32:52.974 --> 00:32:54.313
I mean, a few thoughts,

00:32:54.313 --> 00:32:56.628
the first is for the love of P,

00:32:56.628 --> 00:32:59.239
please ask them about
things other than school,

00:32:59.239 --> 00:33:01.724
because so many kids feel like, you know,

00:33:01.724 --> 00:33:03.871
there's an article I'm
still wanting to get

00:33:03.871 --> 00:33:07.637
some paper that we basically had us,

00:33:07.637 --> 00:33:08.674
hmm, let's take her,

00:33:08.674 --> 00:33:09.771
how was school?

00:33:09.771 --> 00:33:10.633
How are your grades?

00:33:10.633 --> 00:33:11.698
Where are you applying to college?

00:33:11.698 --> 00:33:12.873
Do you have a boyfriend, a girlfriend?

00:33:12.873 --> 00:33:15.923
As though that's the sum total
of their experience, right?

00:33:15.923 --> 00:33:20.026
So if you're getting
the fine, stop asking,

00:33:20.026 --> 00:33:21.574
and stop asking about school.

00:33:21.574 --> 00:33:23.921
Okay, but I have a specific to do now.

00:33:23.921 --> 00:33:25.724
I'll come back then a moment.

00:33:25.724 --> 00:33:27.272
Ask about kind of everything else,

00:33:27.272 --> 00:33:28.770
particularly asking about their hobbies.

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:29.688
What are they interested in?

00:33:29.688 --> 00:33:33.397
You know, politics,
everything else in the world.

00:33:33.397 --> 00:33:36.087
Also, if you have a kid where there's,

00:33:36.087 --> 00:33:38.312
you know there's a problem,

00:33:38.312 --> 00:33:40.234
they don't have friends at school, right,

00:33:40.234 --> 00:33:41.215
they're having a hard time.

00:33:41.215 --> 00:33:42.225
Math is a hard thing.

00:33:42.225 --> 00:33:45.472
When we keep asking about
the thing, that's a problem,

00:33:45.472 --> 00:33:47.925
we're kind of interviewing for pain.

00:33:47.925 --> 00:33:50.163
We're looking, and because in many ways,

00:33:50.163 --> 00:33:51.800
what parents I think are trying to do

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:54.063
is they really just wanna
be told that it's okay,

00:33:54.063 --> 00:33:56.199
so they don't have to worry about this.

00:33:56.199 --> 00:33:57.547
But in this situation,

00:33:57.547 --> 00:34:00.010
you're being the opposite
or non-anxious presence.

00:34:00.010 --> 00:34:03.394
You're feeling like I have to
check over and over and over

00:34:03.394 --> 00:34:04.633
and make sure that went out, okay,

00:34:04.633 --> 00:34:05.656
just like, did you do homework,

00:34:05.656 --> 00:34:06.757
did you do your homework,
did you do your homework,

00:34:06.757 --> 00:34:09.026
did you do your homework, did
you do your homework, right?

00:34:09.026 --> 00:34:11.851
It's putting all the
energy into that, right?

00:34:11.851 --> 00:34:13.137
What was for lunch today?

00:34:13.137 --> 00:34:14.751
What was the weirdest thing that happened?

00:34:14.751 --> 00:34:15.584
What's going on?

00:34:15.584 --> 00:34:19.000
I mean, particularly with
the last two years of COVID,

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:20.988
taking an interest in what
your kids are watching

00:34:20.988 --> 00:34:22.536
at TikTok, right?

00:34:22.536 --> 00:34:26.077
If you want your kids to be open with you,

00:34:26.077 --> 00:34:27.614
try to ask them about things

00:34:27.614 --> 00:34:29.510
other than they give you information

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:31.477
and they feel like they're
gonna be in trouble

00:34:31.477 --> 00:34:33.985
or they're gonna be criticized for it.

00:34:33.985 --> 00:34:36.235
- I love, what's the
weirdest thing you saw today?

00:34:36.235 --> 00:34:38.202
That's fun.
- Yeah.

00:34:38.202 --> 00:34:41.369
And if it is something that in school,

00:34:42.727 --> 00:34:45.264
what was the hardest thing
that popped up in chemistry?

00:34:45.264 --> 00:34:46.923
What was the dopiest thing?

00:34:46.923 --> 00:34:48.861
You try to make those
questions a lot more specific

00:34:48.861 --> 00:34:51.171
because, yeah, if you ask how was school?

00:34:51.171 --> 00:34:52.972
Of course they're gonna say, fine,

00:34:52.972 --> 00:34:55.094
because if they say,
well, it was really hard,

00:34:55.094 --> 00:34:56.343
they feel like they're
gonna be interviewed

00:34:56.343 --> 00:34:57.368
or grilled about the thing.

00:34:57.368 --> 00:34:58.281
What, what, wait a second.

00:34:58.281 --> 00:35:00.292
What do you mean you
didn't do all of this?

00:35:00.292 --> 00:35:01.507
They don't wanna hear about this

00:35:01.507 --> 00:35:03.707
because this is really interesting to me,

00:35:03.707 --> 00:35:04.980
in writing this book,

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:07.681
we realized that one of the things is that

00:35:07.681 --> 00:35:12.154
a lot of kids don't want to
share the hard parts of school,

00:35:12.154 --> 00:35:14.818
life, friendships with their parents,

00:35:14.818 --> 00:35:17.405
because they don't wanna
burden their parents.

00:35:17.405 --> 00:35:18.716
They don't wanna get the parents upset

00:35:18.716 --> 00:35:21.729
and it's too much for them to deal

00:35:21.729 --> 00:35:23.968
with their parents' intense feelings

00:35:23.968 --> 00:35:26.468
on top of their hard feelings.

00:35:27.721 --> 00:35:29.072
So this is where kids say, fine,

00:35:29.072 --> 00:35:32.497
even though we know it's not fine.

00:35:32.497 --> 00:35:34.535
So I will share this
particular piece of advice

00:35:34.535 --> 00:35:37.945
if you have a kid where you
know things are not fine,

00:35:37.945 --> 00:35:39.495
but you can't get them to talk to you.

00:35:39.495 --> 00:35:41.922
So I have a daughter who's
a senior in high school

00:35:41.922 --> 00:35:43.412
and she is a complicated bird,

00:35:43.412 --> 00:35:46.662
she's brilliant, 30 IQ points I've got,

00:35:47.507 --> 00:35:48.855
has had her share of dark things.

00:35:48.855 --> 00:35:51.423
This last couple years
have not been easy for her.

00:35:51.423 --> 00:35:53.771
And when she is unhappy,

00:35:53.771 --> 00:35:55.099
she just closes down, you know,

00:35:55.099 --> 00:35:57.774
she's the kid in the cave.

00:35:57.774 --> 00:36:00.010
And so the conversation
will often look like they,

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:02.825
"Hey, kid, are you okay?"

00:36:02.825 --> 00:36:04.761
So she shrugs her shoulders, I'm like,

00:36:04.761 --> 00:36:06.594
"Any I can help with?"

00:36:07.523 --> 00:36:09.561
And I'll ask two or
three questions like that

00:36:09.561 --> 00:36:11.499
and she's not giving it up.

00:36:11.499 --> 00:36:15.007
And in many ways, these are her feelings

00:36:15.007 --> 00:36:16.995
and these are hers to feel.

00:36:16.995 --> 00:36:18.370
It's not my responsibility.

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:20.435
And I know that sounds
like a talent thing to do.

00:36:20.435 --> 00:36:21.268
But honestly,

00:36:21.268 --> 00:36:23.247
it's an important thing
for young people to learn

00:36:23.247 --> 00:36:26.010
to be able to talk themselves
out of hard feelings,

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:28.010
if she can only get back
to baseline because of me,

00:36:28.010 --> 00:36:30.224
I'm not doing my job as a dad.

00:36:30.224 --> 00:36:32.696
So what I've done with her is this,

00:36:32.696 --> 00:36:35.984
is validate and then offer support, right?

00:36:35.984 --> 00:36:38.647
And then step away and
say, "well, sweetheart,

00:36:38.647 --> 00:36:40.887
it sure looks like you had a day of it

00:36:40.887 --> 00:36:42.250
'cause I don't know what's going on,

00:36:42.250 --> 00:36:43.500
but you look really upset.

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:45.185
If there's any way that I can help,

00:36:45.185 --> 00:36:47.977
please know, if you wanna
listen, if you want it,

00:36:47.977 --> 00:36:50.153
whatever I can do to help" right?

00:36:50.153 --> 00:36:51.314
"Is that okay?"

00:36:51.314 --> 00:36:52.577
Shoulder shrug.

00:36:52.577 --> 00:36:54.235
Here's the question that I ask,

00:36:54.235 --> 00:36:56.307
"Is it all right if I circle
back in about half an hour,

00:36:56.307 --> 00:36:59.006
just to check in and make
sure you're doing okay?"

00:36:59.006 --> 00:36:59.958
Get the head nod.

00:36:59.958 --> 00:37:03.262
And I swear, every time I've done this,

00:37:03.262 --> 00:37:04.662
if I say, I'll be back in a half an hour,

00:37:04.662 --> 00:37:06.449
in 20 minutes she's bounced downstairs.

00:37:06.449 --> 00:37:07.472
So I say, I'll come back an hour.

00:37:07.472 --> 00:37:08.771
She comes down in 40 minutes.

00:37:08.771 --> 00:37:10.846
Every single time.

00:37:10.846 --> 00:37:12.659
And she's just, and whatever cloud it is

00:37:12.659 --> 00:37:14.583
that's been in her mind has just passed,

00:37:14.583 --> 00:37:18.671
and not once have I found
out what she was upset about.

00:37:18.671 --> 00:37:19.820
I knew she was upset.

00:37:19.820 --> 00:37:20.896
She knew she was upset,

00:37:20.896 --> 00:37:22.481
but she just didn't wanna share.

00:37:22.481 --> 00:37:24.855
And so this is the way
we as parents kind of,

00:37:24.855 --> 00:37:27.468
we offer support, we offer help for,

00:37:27.468 --> 00:37:29.843
could a lot of times for kids to know

00:37:29.843 --> 00:37:31.854
that we're there to help,

00:37:31.854 --> 00:37:36.271
even if they don't take us
up on the help is helpful.

00:37:38.454 --> 00:37:39.287
- Thank you.

00:37:39.287 --> 00:37:40.231
I'm gonna close on that.

00:37:40.231 --> 00:37:42.019
That seems like,

00:37:42.019 --> 00:37:44.959
that's an excellent
note for us to leave on.

00:37:44.959 --> 00:37:47.654
Thank you so much for joining us today.

00:37:47.654 --> 00:37:50.755
Really appreciated all
the information you shared

00:37:50.755 --> 00:37:54.781
and look forward to
diving into the new book.

00:37:54.781 --> 00:37:55.631
- I hope you enjoy it.

00:37:55.631 --> 00:37:56.731
I really appreciate.

00:37:56.731 --> 00:37:58.391
Again, I love that you,

00:37:58.391 --> 00:38:00.491
I love that the Khan Academy is taking all

00:38:00.491 --> 00:38:02.741
of that kind of technical
stuff you teach everything

00:38:02.741 --> 00:38:05.278
and really trying to broaden
it with some of the folks

00:38:05.278 --> 00:38:07.703
that I'm grateful to be among those.

00:38:07.703 --> 00:38:08.964
So thank you.

00:38:08.964 --> 00:38:10.365
- Absolutely.

00:38:10.365 --> 00:38:11.899
Thanks all for watching today.

00:38:11.899 --> 00:38:13.899
We'll see you next time.

